COMMAND "Ensure a bp completion noun" DESCRIPTION "Idempotently make sure a top-level noun appears in both scaffy-owned copies of the bp CLI noun set — append its quoted literal once to completionNouns (shell completion) and once to usageBuiltins (the top-level usage built-ins line), skip each forever after. The ensure family: each guard keys on the hand-reproducible noun literal itself, so a human who typed the same entry first wins and scaffy stays out of the way." LAST_UPDATED "17-07-2026-13-00-00" DOMAIN "barkpark" TAGS "cli", "scaffold" CONCEPT "ensure-cli-noun" VARIANT "go" DIRECTION "add" VARIABLES
  VARIABLE 1 "Noun" TITLE "Top-level bp noun to make completable" DESCRIPTION "The `bp <noun>` this entry completes — a lowercase single word (the lowercase casings coincide), or a hyphenated verb like `go-live` in kebab. Referenced here only as {{.noun}} (kebab)." EXAMPLES "widget", "go-live", "sheet"

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# THE ENSURE LAW (charter D57) — why this is not a MARK-guarded command
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# An ensure command guarantees a line a developer could just as well have typed
# by hand — here, one `"noun",` entry in completionNouns. So idempotency keys on
# that HAND-REPRODUCIBLE literal, the quoted noun `"{{.noun}}"`, and NEVER on a
# `MARK:` comment. That is the whole distinction from add-oban-worker /
# add-cli-verb, whose guards key on the mark because scaffy OWNS the block it
# plants. Here it does not: a human's completion entry and scaffy's are the same
# `"noun",` line, and the guard must treat them identically.
#
# Two consequences fall out of that law, both binding on every ensure-* command:
#
#   1. A NO-OP RUN WRITES NO RECEIPT. If the guard already sees `"{{.noun}}"`
#      (however it got there — scaffy earlier, or a hand edit), the op is skipped
#      and NOTHING is written: no file change, and no receipt in
#      `.scaffy/receipts/`. Scaffy records only what it actually did.
#
#   2. `bp scaffy remove` AFTER A SKIP EXITS 4 — AND THAT IS CORRECT. remove is
#      receipt replay (D36). A skipped ensure wrote no receipt, so remove finds
#      none and exits 4 (ReceiptMissingError), leaving the file untouched. Scaffy
#      never owned that noun entry, so it must not remove it. Exit 4 here is the
#      SUCCESS signal of the ensure contract, not a failure — do not "fix" it.
#
# QUOTED-LITERAL GUARD, FILE-WIDE (the counterpart to ensure-import's caveat).
# Unlike ensure-import's bare `import Logger` substring — a prefix of
# `import Logger.Handlers` — this guard `"{{.noun}}"` is delimited by a quote on
# BOTH sides, so `"widget"` is NOT a substring of `"widgets"` (the closing quote
# breaks the match). It does NOT suffer the prefix false-skip. The one real edge
# is that the guard is FILE-scoped: if the exact quoted literal `"{{.noun}}"`
# already appears ANYWHERE in builtins.go (a help string, another slice), the op
# false-skips. For plausible nouns this never collides; if it might, add the
# completionNouns entry by hand. Documented, non-issue for the common case.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# WHAT THIS COMMAND DOES NOT TOUCH — the three-copy discipline
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A completable noun lives in THREE places; this command owns TWO of them:
#
#   • completionNouns (builtins.go)       ← op 1 appends here.
#   • the usage built-ins line (usage.go) ← op 2 appends to usageBuiltins, the
#                                            slice the line renders from. (The
#                                            line was hand-maintained prose and
#                                            drifted 27 nouns behind dispatch;
#                                            the 2026-07-17 backfill made it a
#                                            slice — scaffy-backlog-usage-noun-
#                                            drift-fix, closing the W6-measured
#                                            worst duel gap: every catalog-armed
#                                            agent had to hand-top-up usage.go
#                                            after the engine run.)
#   • the `switch noun` dispatch (cli.go) ← HAND-WRITTEN, deliberately not touched.
#
# The dispatch case is left to the human because a `case "{{.noun}}":` needs a
# real handler — code, not a scaffold. The invariants that keep the three in
# sync are TestCompletionNounsCoverAllDispatchedBuiltins (builtins_test.go) and
# its mirror TestUsageBuiltinsCoverAllDispatchedBuiltins (usage_test.go): each
# asserts every DISPATCHED case is present in its slice — SUBSET checks. So a
# SUPERSET is legal in both slices: this command may add its entries BEFORE (or
# without) the matching dispatch case, and both tests stay green. That is
# exactly the probe that proves this command safe — plant the noun, run the
# tests, they pass even with no dispatch case behind it.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# OP 1 — insert `"{{.noun}}",` once, right after the completionNouns opening
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The array's last element `"workspace",` carries a trailing comma, so a bare
# INSERT is comma-safe (A3) — no self-consuming REPLACE, no REANCHOR needed. The
# anchor is the array-OPENING line, which is unique in the file and ordering-
# neutral: completionNouns is a completion SET, so a new entry right after the
# `{` is as valid as one at the tail. Each ensured noun stacks its own guarded
# entry there; order never matters. The payload plants a provenance comment
# carrying the mark (a remove handle + a reader's breadcrumb) plus the entry. The
# mark name is the FIXED kebab `ensure-cli-noun`: a plain MARK requires its
# `MARK:` text verbatim in the payload (it is here), and it need not be per-noun
# unique because remove is receipt replay keyed on command+vars (D35/D36) — two
# ensured nouns in one file get two receipts and retract independently, even
# sharing the mark comment.
#
# The ASLONG guard is the ENSURE LAW in one line: DONT CONTAIN the quoted noun.
# It is a verbatim substring of the payload (E-006, add polarity → guard derives
# from what the op writes), ASCII, and brace-safe (`}}` is followed by `\"`,
# never a literal `}`).
IN "internal/cli/builtins.go"
### Ensure "{{.noun}}" is a completion noun
INSERT AFTER FIRST
::: completionNouns opening :::
var completionNouns = []string{
::: completionNouns opening :::
WITH
::: {{.noun}} completion noun :::
	// scaffy:ensure-cli-noun {{.noun}} MARK:ensure-cli-noun
	"{{.noun}}",
::: {{.noun}} completion noun :::
MARK "ensure-cli-noun"
ASLONG FILE DONT CONTAIN "\"{{.noun}}\""

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# OP 2 — insert `"{{.noun}}",` once, right after the usageBuiltins opening
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The W6-duel gap, closed: the usage "built-ins:" line renders from the
# usageBuiltins slice (usage.go:20, `var usageBuiltins = []string{` — unique in
# the file), so ensuring the noun is one more comma-safe INSERT with the exact
# shape of op 1: every element carries a trailing comma, the anchor is the
# array-opening line, ordering is neutral because usageBuiltinLines() SORTS at
# render time — a new entry at the head is as valid as one at the tail.
#
# The mark name differs from op 1's on purpose: mark uniqueness is pair-scoped
# (D24) — cross-file REUSE of one mark name is legal only between DIRECTION-
# opposed commands, and these two adds live in one command, so each file gets
# its own family name. Same plain-MARK rule as op 1: `MARK:ensure-cli-noun-usage`
# appears verbatim in the payload.
#
# Same ensure-law guard, same FILE-scoped edge as op 1's builtins.go caveat:
# usage.go carries two pre-existing single-word quoted literals, `"set"` and
# `"bool"` (writeBodyHint / the flag-type check) — ensuring a noun literally
# named `set` or `bool` would false-skip here. No plausible CLI noun collides;
# if one ever does, add the usageBuiltins entry by hand. And after the
# 2026-07-17 backfill every ALREADY-dispatched noun is present in the slice, so
# ensuring an existing noun no-ops in both files — the contract's end state.
IN "internal/cli/usage.go"
### Ensure "{{.noun}}" is on the usage built-ins line
INSERT AFTER FIRST
::: usageBuiltins opening :::
var usageBuiltins = []string{
::: usageBuiltins opening :::
WITH
::: {{.noun}} usage noun :::
	// scaffy:ensure-cli-noun {{.noun}} MARK:ensure-cli-noun-usage
	"{{.noun}}",
::: {{.noun}} usage noun :::
MARK "ensure-cli-noun-usage"
ASLONG FILE DONT CONTAIN "\"{{.noun}}\""

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# POSTCONDITIONS
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The quoted noun is present in BOTH scaffy-owned copies (verbatim substring of
# the payload each op writes). This holds whether scaffy inserted the entry this
# run or a prior run / a hand edit already had it — the ensure contract
# guarantees the END STATE.
ASSERT FILE "internal/cli/builtins.go" CONTAINS "\"{{.noun}}\""
ASSERT FILE "internal/cli/usage.go" CONTAINS "\"{{.noun}}\""

# Drift gate — the completion/dispatch AND usage/dispatch invariants in one
# run (`-run` is a regex; the shared suffix matches both
# TestCompletionNounsCoverAllDispatchedBuiltins and
# TestUsageBuiltinsCoverAllDispatchedBuiltins). A LOCAL Go test (A2:
# proven-safe): adding entries keeps both green because each check is
# dispatched-⊆-slice; a superset never reds. Runs in the apply worktree; the
# engine appends CC=/usr/bin/clang for cgo.
ASSERT CMD "go test ./internal/cli/ -run CoverAllDispatchedBuiltins -count=1"
